  <!-- Section 9: How to Switch from China to Bangladesh -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">How to Switch from China to Bangladesh | Step by Step</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Transitioning manufacturing from China to Bangladesh doesn't happen overnight, but the process is manageable with proper planning. Follow these steps to minimize disruption while capturing cost savings and risk reduction. The timeline from initial contact to first Bangladesh delivery typically spans 8-12 weeks.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Step 1: Audit Current China Setup</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Review your current China manufacturing costs in detail. Calculate FOB price per unit, shipping costs, insurance, customs clearance fees, duties, tariffs, and any hidden expenses like inspection or audit costs. Determine your total landed cost per SKU. Identify which SKUs have the highest tariff exposure or recurring quality issues. This audit establishes your baseline for comparison and highlights the highest-priority products for transition.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Step 2: Identify SKUs to Move</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Select product categories suitable for Bangladesh manufacturing. Knitwear including t-shirts, hoodies, sweatshirts, polo shirts, and casual pants transfer well with minimal adjustment. Complex technical outerwear, tailored jackets, or highly specialized items may remain in China initially. Start with 2-3 SKUs that represent your highest volume or highest tariff burden. This phased approach limits risk while allowing you to build confidence in the Bangladesh partnership.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Step 3: Request Samples</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Send your complete tech packs - including specifications, measurements, fabric requirements, and reference samples - to Bangladesh manufacturers. Request pre-production samples that match your specifications precisely. Most Bangladesh factories provide samples within 7-14 days. Review samples against your China production for fabric quality, construction, stitching, and finishing. <a href="/contact" class="link">Request a free sample</a> from SDF to compare quality directly. Don't proceed to bulk until samples meet your standards.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Step 4: Compare Quality</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Conduct a side-by-side quality comparison between your current China samples and the Bangladesh samples. Test fabric durability, color fastness, and shrinkage. Examine stitching consistency, seam strength, and overall construction. Have your quality team or a third-party inspector evaluate both samples objectively. If Bangladesh samples match or exceed China quality, you've identified a viable alternative. If quality falls short, provide specific feedback and request revisions before proceeding.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Step 5: Negotiate Terms</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Finalize pricing, MOQ requirements, lead times, and payment terms with your Bangladesh manufacturer. Ensure the total landed cost including duties is lower than your China baseline. Negotiate payment terms that reduce your risk - preferably 30% deposit, 40% against shipping documents, and 30% after quality inspection. Confirm production capacity and delivery timelines. Document all terms in a comprehensive manufacturing agreement that specifies quality standards, delivery requirements, and dispute resolution procedures.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Step 6: Transition Production</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Start with a pilot order of your selected 2-3 SKUs. Maintain parallel China production for these SKUs initially as a backup. Monitor Bangladesh production quality and on-time delivery closely. Conduct in-line inspections and final inspection before shipment. Once the pilot order arrives and passes your quality check, begin transitioning additional SKUs. Gradually reduce China production as confidence in the Bangladesh partnership grows. This measured transition minimizes disruption while capturing savings.
      </p>

      <p class="section__text">
        Brands that follow this transition process typically achieve 15-30% cost reduction within 6 months while maintaining or improving quality. For <a href="/clothing-manufacturers-for-startups" class="link">startup clothing brands</a>, the MOQ reduction alone can represent $20,000-50,000 in annual savings by reducing inventory requirements. The key is patience - don't rush the transition, but don't delay indefinitely either. With the LDC deadline approaching in November 2026, brands that establish Bangladesh production now secure competitive advantages for years to come.
      </p>
