  </section>

  <!-- Section 10: FAQ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">FAQ | China Clothing Manufacturing</h2>
      
      <dl class="faq-list">
        <dt class="faq-question">How much does it cost to manufacture clothing in China?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Manufacturing costs in China vary by product type and complexity. Basic t-shirts typically cost $3.50-5.00 FOB per piece. Hoodies range from $8-12 FOB. Jackets can cost $15-25 FOB. These prices exclude shipping, duties, and tariffs. China's labor costs have risen significantly since 2010, with average garment worker wages now $400-600 monthly in coastal manufacturing cities. When calculating total landed cost, add shipping (typically 5-10% of FOB), customs clearance fees, duties (MFN rates of 12-16%), and Section 301 tariffs (25% for most categories). The actual landed cost per t-shirt often reaches $6-8 after all fees are included.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">What is the minimum order quantity for Chinese clothing factories?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Most Chinese clothing manufacturers require 500-2,000 pieces per style as minimum order quantity. Large factories often require 1,000+ pieces per style and sometimes 500-1,000 pieces per color. This high MOQ makes China challenging for startup brands testing new designs. Some smaller factories accept 300-500 pieces but at higher per-unit costs - often 20-30% above standard rates. The MOQ requirement stems from China's factory infrastructure, which is optimized for large-scale production. Brands with smaller orders face either higher prices or difficulty finding willing manufacturers. Bangladesh factories typically offer lower MOQs (300-1,000 pieces) at competitive pricing, making them more accessible to emerging brands.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">How do US tariffs affect the cost of importing from China?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          US Section 301 tariffs add 25% to most clothing imports from China. Some categories face even higher rates up to 145%. For example, a $10,000 shipment from China incurs $2,500 in additional duty. Bangladesh pays standard MFN duties of 15-32% but avoids Section 301 surcharges. This tariff difference can represent 15-40% higher landed costs for Chinese imports. The tariffs apply to the Customs Value (FOB price plus shipping costs to US port), not just the factory price. A $10,000 FOB shipment with $2,000 in shipping costs faces a $3,000 tariff (25% of $12,000). For brands importing $100,000 annually from China, Section 301 tariffs alone add $25,000 in costs. These tariffs have been in place since 2018 with no clear timeline for removal, creating permanent cost disadvantages for China-sourced apparel.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">Is it safe to manufacture clothing in China as a startup?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Manufacturing in China presents risks for startups including intellectual property concerns, high MOQ requirements, and tariff unpredictability. The USTR annual reports document ongoing IP enforcement challenges in China. However, thousands of brands successfully manufacture in China by working with verified factories, using strong contracts, and conducting regular quality inspections. The key risks include: (1) IP theft or unauthorized production runs, (2) quality inconsistencies if you lack on-ground oversight, (3) payment disputes if you pay large deposits without milestones, (4) tariff increases that disrupt your pricing model, and (5) communication challenges due to time zones and language barriers. Startups can mitigate these risks through third-party audits, comprehensive NDAs, milestone-based payments, and working with established export-oriented factories. However, many startups find that Bangladesh or Vietnam offer lower-risk alternatives with similar quality for basic to mid-range products.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">How do I verify a Chinese clothing manufacturer is legitimate?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Verify Chinese manufacturers through multiple channels: check business license on China's National Enterprise Credit Information System, request third-party audit reports from BSCI or SEDEX, verify Alibaba Gold Supplier status and trade assurance, request client references, conduct factory visits or video tours, and start with small sample orders before committing to bulk production. Red flags include refusal to provide business licenses, unverifiable certifications, demands for large upfront deposits (50%+), pressure to use personal bank accounts instead of company accounts, and inability to provide recent client references. Legitimate factories are transparent about their operations, welcome verification, and have documented track records. Consider hiring a local sourcing agent or quality inspection company to conduct on-site verification if you cannot visit personally. The investment in verification saves far more than it costs by avoiding problematic partnerships.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">What certifications should a Chinese clothing manufacturer have?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Key certifications include ISO 9001 for quality management, BSCI or SEDEX for social compliance, OEKO-TEX for chemical safety, GOTS for organic cotton, and WRAP for ethical manufacturing. For US markets, verify C-TPAT certification for supply chain security. Always verify certificates directly with issuing organizations - certificates can be forged. Chinese factories targeting export markets typically hold 3-5 certifications. However, certification quality varies - some factories maintain certifications only on paper without implementing actual standards. Request recent audit reports to verify that certifications reflect actual practices. For organic or sustainable products, GOTS and OEKO-TEX are particularly important as these certifications require rigorous supply chain traceability. Bangladesh factories often hold more comprehensive certification coverage, particularly for sustainable materials, due to EU market requirements.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">Is Bangladesh cheaper than China for clothing manufacturing?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Bangladesh is typically 15-30% cheaper than China for clothing manufacturing. Labor costs in Bangladesh average $100-150 monthly compared to China's $400-600. EU buyers save an additional 12% duty through Bangladesh's duty-free EBA access until November 2026. For US buyers, Bangladesh avoids Section 301 tariffs that add 25% to Chinese imports. On a $10,000 FOB shipment, total landed cost to the US might be $13,500 from Bangladesh (FOB + shipping + 16.5% duty) versus $16,500 from China (FOB + shipping + 16.5% duty + 25% Section 301 tariff). The $3,000 savings represents 18% cost reduction. The advantage compounds at scale - brands importing $100,000 annually save $30,000 by switching to Bangladesh. While China may offer lower FOB prices for some complex items, the tariff and duty differences typically make Bangladesh more cost-effective for basic to mid-range knitwear.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">How long does shipping take from China vs Bangladesh?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Sea freight from China to US West Coast takes 15-20 days. To Europe, China takes 28-35 days. From Bangladesh, sea freight to US West Coast takes 22-28 days. To Europe, Bangladesh takes 22-28 days. Production lead times are comparable: 30-45 days in both countries. Bangladesh is geographically closer to Europe, offering slightly shorter shipping times. For US brands, China has a 7-10 day shipping advantage to the West Coast. However, the total time from order placement to delivery (production + shipping) is often similar: 50-65 days from China versus 55-70 days from Bangladesh. The 5-10 day difference rarely outweighs the cost savings from Bangladesh. Air freight is available from both countries for urgent orders but costs 5-10x more than sea freight. For most brands, the slight shipping time difference from Bangladesh is acceptable given the significant cost savings.
        </dd>
      </dl>
